{"authored" [{"question" "q-98553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+∇f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=-2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & ={e}^{y}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=2, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =x{e}^{y} &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=-1{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =⟨2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+∇\\,f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨2,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ \\\\  & =-2+2(x+1)-2(y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5ec23d17fb67e890f41a65ae1ac28efe96d7fe546dc24470d93799900dc2cff6" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98553" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98553" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y)≈-2+2x-2y"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98553" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y)≈-2-x+y"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98553" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x+y"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98553" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-98553" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-x-y"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/state.json" "file_sha256" "cd56eab8b5bc9f63d984b6c9ea8be9d43a273cac3967932c65823073636be38c" "basis" 1859 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057928/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-98342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,2,3)=3,\\,{f}_{x}(1,2,3)=-2,\\,{f}_{y}(1,2,3)=1,\\,{f}_{z}(1,2,3)=3.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ estimate the value of $f(0.96,1.98,3.03).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-98342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,2,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,2,3)+∇f(1,2,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,2,3)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,2,3),{f}_{y}(1,2,3),{f}_{z}(1,2,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩ \\\\  & =3+⟨-2,1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-2,z-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(0.96,1.98,3.03)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.96,$ $y=1.98,$ and $z=3.03$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.96,1.98,3.03) & ≈3+⟨-2,1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0.96-1,1.98-2,3.03-3⟩ \\\\  & =3+⟨-2,1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.04,-0.02,0.03⟩ \\\\  & =3+0.08-0.02+0.09 \\\\  & =3.15\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-98342" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "41f3150d516119df399669f3a9fe831b1007fdbde3ed9270a4d52032d4aae1b4"} {"question" "q-98342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-98342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2.99"] ["math" "3.01"] ["math" "3.03"] ["math" "3.15"] ["math" "3.25"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-98342" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3.15"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-98347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & f(3,0,4)=25, &  & {f}_{x}(x,y,z)=\\frac{5x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}, \\\\  & {f}_{y}(x,y,z)=\\frac{5y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}, &  & {f}_{z}(x,y,z)=\\frac{5z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,0,4),$ estimate the value of $f(3.01,0.1,3.99).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-98347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(3,0,4)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(3,0,4)+∇f(3,0,4)\\cdot ⟨x-3,y-0,z-4⟩ \\\\  & =25+⟨{f}_{x}(3,0,4),{f}_{y}(3,0,4),{f}_{z}(3,0,4)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y,z-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(3,0,4)$, ${f}_{y}(3,0,4)$ and ${f}_{z}(3,0,4),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(3,0,4) & =\\frac{5(3)}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}=3 \\\\ {f}_{y}(3,0,4) & =\\frac{5(0)}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}=0 \\\\ {f}_{z}(3,0,4) & =\\frac{5(4)}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈25+⟨3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨x-3,y,z-4⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(3.01,0.1,3.99)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=3.01,y=0.1$ and $z=3.99,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(3.01,0.1,3.99) & ≈25+⟨3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨3.01-3,0.1,3.99-4⟩ \\\\  & =25+⟨3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.1,-0.01⟩ \\\\  & =25+0.03-0.04 \\\\  & =24.99\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-98347" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "41f3150d516119df399669f3a9fe831b1007fdbde3ed9270a4d52032d4aae1b4"} {"question" "q-98347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-98347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "24.78"] ["math" "24.85"] ["math" "24.99"] ["math" "25.06"] ["math" "25.64"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-98347" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24.99"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-98553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-98553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+∇f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=-2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & ={e}^{y}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=2, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =x{e}^{y} &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=-1{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =⟨2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)+∇\\,f(-1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨2,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,\\,y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩ \\\\  & =-2+2(x+1)-2(y-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-98553" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "41f3150d516119df399669f3a9fe831b1007fdbde3ed9270a4d52032d4aae1b4"} {"question" "q-98553" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-98553" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x,y)≈-2+2x-2y"] ["math" "f(x,y)≈-2-x+y"] ["math" "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x+y"] ["math" "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y"] ["math" "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-x-y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-98553" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y)≈2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+2x-2y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-129654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,0,-1)=1,\\,{f}_{x}(1,0,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{y}(1,0,-1)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(1,0,-1)=0.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,-1)$ estimate the value of $f(1.02,0.1,-0.96)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-129654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,0,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,0,-1)+∇f(1,0,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-0,z-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,0,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,0,-1),{f}_{y}(1,0,-1),{f}_{z}(1,0,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z+1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.02,0.1,-0.96)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.02,$ $y=0.1,$ and $z=-0.96$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.02,0.1,-0.96) & ≈1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1.02-1,0.1,-0.96+1⟩ \\\\  & =1+⟨-1,-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0.02,0.1,0.04⟩ \\\\  & =1-1(0.02)-1(0.1)+0(0.04) \\\\  & =1-0.02-0.1+0 \\\\  & =0.88\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-129654" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "41f3150d516119df399669f3a9fe831b1007fdbde3ed9270a4d52032d4aae1b4"} {"question" "q-129654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-129654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.88"] ["math" "0.93"] ["math" "0.98"] ["math" "1.03"] ["math" "1.09"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-129654" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.88"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-129746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(x,y,z)=yz-x.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,-1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-129746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,-1,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,-1,1)+∇f(0,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x,y+1,z-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(0,-1,1)=-1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =-1\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(0,-1,1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =z\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(0,-1,1)=1, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =y\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(0,-1,1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(0,-1,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(0,-1,1) & =⟨{f}_{x}(0,-1,1),{f}_{y}(0,-1,1),{f}_{z}(0,-1,1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,-1,1)+∇f(0,-1,1)\\cdot ⟨x,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1+⟨-1,1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y+1,z-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-1-(x)+(y+1)-(z-1) \\\\  & ≈1-x+y-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-129746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "41f3150d516119df399669f3a9fe831b1007fdbde3ed9270a4d52032d4aae1b4"} {"question" "q-129746" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-129746" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x,y,z)≈-2+2y+3z"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)≈-3+2x-y+z"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)≈-x-y-2z"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)≈1-x+y-z"] ["math" "f(x,y,z)≈2x+y"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"} {"question" "q-129746" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x,y,z)≈1-x+y-z"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14069727/state.json" "file_sha256" "588917aad60deffa8a19f3a2a6885dfd77c713110de428e7d6dbe33c286e4cfe"}] "usage" {"q-98553" []} "basis" 2405 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186335383 17592186335384 17592186335385 17592186335386 17592186335387] "immutable_fields" [[17592186335382 :answer-field/correct] [17592186335382 :answer-field/id] [17592186335382 :answer-field/key] [17592186335382 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
